<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         WITHOUT THIS THE PAGE LOADS AND THE GAME NEVER APPEARS.

         Avalonia draws through Skia, and in WebAssembly Skia is not a library that can be
         loaded at run time - there is no dynamic linker to load it with. libSkiaSharp.a
         has to be RELINKED INTO THE WASM BINARY at build time, and that only happens when
         this is true.

         With it false the build SUCCEEDS, emits a warning nobody reads -

             @(NativeFileReference) is not empty, but the native references won't be
             linked in, because neither $(WasmBuildNative), nor $(RunAOTCompilation)
             are 'true'

         - and produces a page that shows the Avalonia splash, then dies in the browser
         console with `System.DllNotFoundException: libSkiaSharp` at SKImageInfo's static
         constructor. Nothing on the page says so. That is how this head sat "built" and
         unrunnable: the only signal was a warning in a build that reported 0 errors.

         THE COST IS A WORKLOAD. Relinking needs Emscripten, which arrives with
         `dotnet workload install wasm-tools`; without it the build falls back to the
         warning above rather than failing. See BROWSER.md.
         ================================================================== -->
    <WasmBuildNative>true</WasmBuildNative>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         WITHOUT THIS, SAVING AND SETTINGS THROW - AND THEY THROW SILENTLY.

         The WebAssembly SDK turns OFF reflection-based System.Text.Json, because reflection
         is what trimming cannot see through. Every JSON call in this port is the plain
         `JsonSerializer.Serialize(value, options)` kind, so on this head every one of them
         raised

             InvalidOperationException: JsonSerializerIsReflectionDisabled

         SaveStore.Write and SettingsStore.Write catch IOException, UnauthorizedAccess,
         NotSupported and Security - the things a DISK does - and this is none of them, so it
         went straight past them, out of the caller, and into the browser console where
         nobody was looking. The visible symptom was that saving a game appeared to work and
         the save was simply never there afterwards.

         IT WAS WARNED ABOUT AND THE WARNING WAS READ AND SHELVED. Seven IL2026 trim warnings
         name these exact call sites, every publish, and they were noted as "unverified,
         could fail" for days. They were not unverified; they were unexamined.

         THE PROPER FIX IS A SOURCE-GENERATED JsonSerializerContext over the save, settings
         and network types, which would delete the warnings rather than silence the symptom
         and would trim better. That is a change to Jones.Core and Jones.Net and deserves its
         own run at it. This is the supported switch that makes the existing code work, and
         it is honest about what it costs: the reflection stack stays in the download.
         ================================================================== -->
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>true</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Jones.App\Jones.App.csproj" />

    <!-- The synthesiser. Music and effects are generated in managed code rather than
         played from a device, which is the only reason this head can have them at all. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Jones.Audio\Jones.Audio.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       THE ART, PUBLISHED BESIDE THE PAGE.

       Without it this head draws the 838 cels of Sierra's 1x art compiled into
       Jones.App - which is what it did for its whole existence, because a head that
       finds no art falls back silently and falling back looks exactly like working.

       NOT EMBEDDED, NOT BUNDLED, JUST FILES. The 4x set is 57MB across 1203 cels and a
       player opens a handful of screens, so HttpArtStore fetches each one the first time
       it is drawn. Embedding them in the assembly would put all 57MB in the initial
       download; bundling them in a zip would mean fetching and holding all of it to read
       any of it.

       BOTH DIRECTORIES OR NEITHER. `png4x-cast0` holds the cels we have rebuilt and
       `png4x` holds all 1071; a set is silent about what it does not replace and the
       resolver falls through to the ported copy, so shipping the set alone would draw
       132 cels at 4x beside 939 at 1x.

       THE MANIFEST IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. HTTP has no directory listing, and a set is
       silent about nine cels in ten, so without it every miss would cost a round trip to
       learn that the art set does not cover that cel. One text file, written here,
       read once at startup.
       ================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <JonesAssetRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\..\assets\</JonesAssetRoot>
    <JonesArtOut>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)wwwroot\art\</JonesArtOut>
    <JonesArtManifest>$(JonesArtOut)art-manifest.txt</JonesArtManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <JonesArtFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)png4x\*.png"       ArtDir="png4x" />
    <JonesArtFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)png4x-cast0\*.png" ArtDir="png4x-cast0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THIS PROJECT FILE IS AN INPUT, and leaving it out is a real bug that has already
    shipped once in this repo: with only the files as inputs, REMOVING a line leaves every
    remaining input older than the output, so MSBuild judges the target up to date and
    publishes the previous set. See the Android csproj for the incident.
  -->
  <Target Name="PublishJonesArt" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
          Inputs="@(JonesArtFile);$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)" Outputs="$(JonesArtManifest)">
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(JonesArtOut)" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(JonesArtFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(JonesArtFile->'$(JonesArtOut)%(ArtDir)\%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(JonesArtManifest)"
                      Lines="@(JonesArtFile->'%(ArtDir)/%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
                      Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       THE GAME'S TEXT, SUBTITLES AND MUSIC — THE OTHER HALF OF THE HEAD.

       THE ART WAS PUBLISHED AND NOTHING ELSE WAS, and the consequence was a game that
       looked finished and was hollow. The desktop head calls `SciText.Load`,
       `Subtitles.Load` and `LipSync.SetDirectory` at startup; the Android head does the
       same through `JonesRuntime`. THIS HEAD CALLED NONE OF THEM, because the files were
       not here to call them with.

       What that cost, all from one omission: the Statistics page drew its frame and its
       DONE button over an EMPTY WHITE SHEET, because every line on it is a string in
       text 2xx. No speech balloon ever appeared, because the balloon shows a subtitle and
       `Subtitles.Load` had found no file. And nothing made a sound.

       SMALL ENOUGH TO SHIP WHOLE: 41 text resources at 82 KB, one subtitles file at 36 KB,
       34 MIDI scores at 162 KB. The 22 MB of speech is NOT here and is the next job; it
       belongs on the art's lazy path, not in the initial download.

       NOT `raw\font` — the bitmap faces are already compiled into Jones.App as
       AvaloniaResource, which is how the interface draws before any of this arrives.
       ================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <JonesDataOut>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)wwwroot\res\</JonesDataOut>
    <JonesDataManifest>$(JonesDataOut)data-manifest.txt</JonesDataManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <JonesDataFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)raw\text\*.text"     ResDir="raw/text" />
    <JonesDataFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)audio\subtitles.json" ResDir="audio" />
    <JonesDataFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)audio\midi\*"         ResDir="audio/midi" />
    <JonesDataFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)audio\sync\*"         ResDir="audio/sync" />

    <!-- THE SPEECH IS COPIED BUT NOT LISTED, and the distinction is the whole design.
         Everything in the manifest is fetched at startup; 22 MB of voices is not. These
         are asked for one at a time, by URL, when a shopkeeper actually speaks - the same
         bargain the art makes, for the same reason. -->
    <JonesSpeechFile Include="$(JonesAssetRoot)audio\speech\*.wav" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The project file is an input for the same reason it is one above: without it,
       REMOVING a line leaves every remaining input older than the output and MSBuild
       publishes the previous set. -->
  <Target Name="PublishJonesData" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
          Inputs="@(JonesDataFile);$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)" Outputs="$(JonesDataManifest)">
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(JonesDataOut)" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(JonesDataFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(JonesDataFile->'$(JonesDataOut)%(ResDir)\%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(JonesSpeechFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(JonesSpeechFile->'$(JonesDataOut)audio\speech\%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(JonesDataManifest)"
                      Lines="@(JonesDataFile->'%(ResDir)/%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
                      Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- NO <Content Include="wwwroot\art\**"> HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.

       The WebAssembly SDK already globs wwwroot into Content, so declaring it again is a
       duplicate - NETSDK1022, which the Debug BUILD tolerates and `publish` refuses. The
       art lands under wwwroot above and is carried to the publish output by that default
       glob; the only thing an explicit item adds is a build that works until you try to
       deploy it. -->
</Project>
